     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks unsafe.\n\nMetformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is twice the usual maximum of 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release metformin, and about 2,000 mg/day is the usual ceiling for most patients. A dose that high raises the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nThe intended order may be one of these:\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum.\n- **1000 mg PO twice daily with meals.** This is 2,000 mg/day.\n- **500 mg or 1000 mg PO** at some other frequency, for example when starting or titrating.\n\nTell me the dose you meant, or confirm that 1700 mg three times daily is intentional. If it is, I'll need a clinical rationale, and I'd suggest a pharmacist review before I enter it. Once I have that, I'll enter the order."
      }
     ],
